          <h2>How it turned out, and what I would change</h2>
          <p class="section-lede"><strong>Picking the accessible typeface and the 44px minimum tap target when I first started took no extra time.</strong> Doing it in the last week would have meant redrawing all eight pages, because a bigger typeface rewraps every line and a bigger button changes every row it sits in. Nobody whose grip has actually changed has seen this yet, so organizing the store by task is still only my guess that it would help. Testing it with those people is the first thing I would do if this were a real store.</p>
          <p class="section-lede">What I took away is how much a design system changes what you are willing to try. Once the tokens existed I could change the look of all eight pages in about a minute, so I experimented far more than I would have if every change meant editing every screen by hand.</p>
          <p class="section-lede">I enjoyed this one more than anything else in this portfolio, and I am also the least sure about it. The variables and the components all work. Who the store is for, and the idea that people shop by task, are things I decided on my own. <strong>Spending ten weeks on something this detailed for people I never spoke to felt strange the whole way through</strong>, and that is why every assumption I made is written on this page instead of left out.</p>
          <h2 class="ai-heading">How I would change this today with AI</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">The whole store is built on a guess: that people shop by the task they can no longer do rather than by product type. Today I would test that wording before building anything. A model can generate fifty ways to phrase the four task categories out of real product descriptions, and I could put those in front of people to find out which words they recognize. I would use one to draft the product descriptions in plain language too, then check every one against the reading rules I set for the page.</p>
          <p class="section-lede"><strong>I would not use a model as a substitute for the research I did not do.</strong> It can produce a convincing person whose grip has changed, and that person would be fiction. The gap on this project is that nobody with a real hand injury has used it, and generating an opinion about them does not close that gap.</p>
